---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import ProjectFacts from "../../../components/projects/ProjectFacts.astro";
import ProjectFact from "../../../components/projects/ProjectFact.astro";

// Plain ASCII on purpose: box-drawing glyphs aren't in the font's latin
// subset and would fall back to another font, breaking the alignment.
const diagram = `      someone signs up with
    totovo342@mailinator.com
                |
   +------------v------------+
   |     your signup form    |
   +------------+------------+
                |  GET /check/:email
   +------------v------------+
   |          unfake         |
   |   disposable?  alias?   |
   |    role account?  mx?   |
   |     public provider?    |
   +------------+------------+
                |
        +-------+-------+
        |               |
  allowed: true   allowed: false
   let them in      stop them`;
---

<ProjectLayout project="unfake">
	<section class="intro">
		<p>
			Unfake is an API that tells you whether an email address is disposable
			before it gets into your product. One request, and you know whether
			<code>totovo342@mailinator.com</code> is a real person or an inbox that
			will be gone in ten minutes.
		</p>
		<p>
			I launched it in January 2025 to fix a problem I had on
			<a href="/projects/ipoalerts/" data-umami-event="unfake-ipoalerts">ipoalerts</a>,
			and these days it screens signups there and on a few other websites.
		</p>
	</section>

	<ProjectFacts>
		<ProjectFact label="what">an API that spots disposable and temporary emails</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="checks">
			disposable domains, role accounts, aliases, public providers & MX records
		</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="database">
			200,000+ disposable domains, updated every few minutes
		</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="used by">
			<a href="/projects/ipoalerts/" data-umami-event="unfake-fact-ipoalerts">ipoalerts</a>
			&
			a few other websites
		</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="since">January 2025</ProjectFact>
		<ProjectFact label="status">live</ProjectFact>
	</ProjectFacts>

	<h2>Why I built it</h2>

	<p>
		As ipoalerts grew, so did a less exciting number: a big share of new
		signups came from disposable, temporary inboxes. They cluttered the user
		data, used up resources meant for real users, and made it cheap to game
		the free tier. In October 2024, I caught one person creating account after
		account just to stack up free API keys and run a round-robin.
	</p>
	<p>
		This isn't a small-site problem; Facebook and Twitter have fought fake
		signups for years. Open-source blocklists exist, but they're only as fresh
		as their last update, and new throwaway domains show up <strong>every hour</strong>. I
		wanted something I could call from a signup form and trust, so I built
		Unfake and put it in front of ipoalerts' signups.
	</p>
	<p>
		Every product with a signup form has the same problem, so I built it as
		a product anyone can use, not just a patch for ipoalerts.
	</p>

	<h2>How it works</h2>

	<p>
		Send an email address or a domain to <code>GET /check/:input</code>, and
		Unfake runs it through five checks:
	</p>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Disposable.</strong> Is the domain on the list of 200,000+ known
			throwaway domains, from providers like mailinator.com, yopmail.com and
			temp-mail.org?
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Role account.</strong> Is it a shared inbox such as
			<code>admin@</code> or <code>support@</code>?
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Alias.</strong> Does it use plus addressing, like
			<code>name+test@</code>?
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Public provider.</strong> Is it a free inbox from Gmail, Hotmail
			and the like?
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>MX records.</strong> Can the domain receive email at all?
		</li>
	</ul>

	<pre
		class="diagram"
		role="img"
		aria-label="Someone signs up with a mailinator.com address. Your signup form calls Unfake's check endpoint, which checks whether the address is disposable, an alias, a role account, a public provider, or has no MX records, and answers allowed true to let them in or allowed false to stop them."
	>{diagram}</pre>

	<p>
		You decide which checks count. Out of the box, Unfake only blocks
		disposable addresses, and every other check can be switched on per
		request. A B2B app can turn away gmail.com, while a consumer app lets it
		straight through. The response has a single <code>allowed</code> field for
		the common case, and the result of every check for when you want the
		details.
	</p>

	<h2>Where it is today</h2>

	<p>
		Unfake is live. Besides ipoalerts, it guards signups on a few other websites.
	</p>
	<p>
		The database tracks over 200,000 disposable domains and is updated every
		few minutes. Users report new throwaway providers almost every day, and
		scripts fold them in. For bigger setups, there are custom rules per API
		key, your own allow and block lists, and bulk checks of up to 50,000
		emails at a time.
	</p>
	<p>There are also a few free tools on the site:</p>

	<ul>
		<li>
			<a href="https://unfake.dev/validate-email" data-umami-event="unfake-validate-email">Email address validator</a>,
			to check a single address.
		</li>
		<li>
			<a href="https://unfake.dev/clean-email-lists" data-umami-event="unfake-clean-lists">Email list cleaner</a>,
			to weed throwaway addresses out of a list you already have.
		</li>
		<li>
			<a href="https://unfake.dev/compare-blocklists" data-umami-event="unfake-compare">Blocklist comparison</a>,
			to see how Unfake stacks up against the popular open-source blocklists
			on GitHub, one address at a time.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Using Unfake</h2>

	<p>
		Create an account at
		<a href="https://unfake.dev/" data-umami-event="unfake-site">unfake.dev</a>,
		grab an API key from the dashboard, and add one request to your signup
		flow. The <a href="https://unfake.dev/docs" data-umami-event="unfake-docs">docs</a>
		cover the rest.
	</p>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
	.intro {
		margin: 2rem 0;
	}

	p,
	li {
		font-size:   1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
	}

	li + li {
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}

	h2 {
		margin-top: 2.5rem;
	}

	/* Scales down on small screens so all 36 columns fit, no scrolling. */
	.diagram {
		margin:           1.5rem 0;
		padding:          1rem 0.75rem;
		background-color: var(--code-bg);
		border-radius:    4px;
		font-size:        min(0.8rem, 2.9vw);
		line-height:      1.35;
	}
</style>
